Instructions Implemented
------------------------
Branch: BEQ, BNE, BGTZ, BLEZ, BLTZ, BGEZ, BLTZAL, BGEZAL
Jump: J, JAL, JR, JALR
Shift (immediate): SLL, SRL, SRA
Shift (variable): SLLV, SRLV, SRAV
ALU (immediate): ADDI, ADDIU, SLTI, SLTIU, ANDI, ORI, XORI, LUI
ALU (register): ADD, ADDU, SUB, SUBU, AND, OR, XOR, NOR, SLT, SLTU, MUL
Load: LB, LBU, LH, LW, LL
Store: SB, SH, SW, SC
Conditional move: MOVN, MOVZ

Hazard Handling and Forwarding
-------------------------------
Forwarding is implemented in the decode stage by comparing the source register
addresses (rs, rt) against the destination register addresses of instructions
currently in the EX and MEM stages.

EX-stage forwarding applies when an instruction in EX will write a register
that the instruction in ID is reading, provided the EX instruction is not a
load (the load result is not available until after the MEM stage). In that
case the ALU result from EX is forwarded directly into the rs_data or rt_data
paths used by the decode stage.

MEM-stage forwarding applies when an instruction in MEM will write a register
that the instruction in ID is reading. The writeback data from MEM is
forwarded in place of the register file read. EX-stage forwarding takes
priority over MEM-stage forwarding.

Load-use stalls insert one bubble when the instruction in EX is a load and
the immediately following instruction reads the destination of that load
(either as rs or as rt). The pipeline is stalled by disabling the fetch stage
enable and injecting a NOP into the ID/EX pipeline registers for one cycle.
The saved instruction register holds the stalled instruction so it can be
re-decoded on the next cycle.

For branch instructions, the branch condition is evaluated in the decode stage
using forwarded register values, so branches resolve in ID with no branch
delay beyond the single branch delay slot that the assembler fills.

AI Usage
--------
GPT was used to understand the problem setup, map instruction timing across
pipeline stages, and interpret waveform diagrams during debugging.
